l is in requirement_states with ready requirements, while sort doesn't have any ready requirements yet.\n\nLet me verify the timeline here. Looking at all the SRDs in requirement_states, srd004-ts appears in 15.0, srd055-tail is in 07.0, and everything else starts from 07.1 onward. So 07.0 is definitely the earliest release with ready work available. Checking srd055-tail specifically, all eight requirements across R3 and R4 are marked ready.\n\nNow I need to figure out the weights for these requirements. The instructions say each task's total weight can't exceed 4, and I should pull weights from requirements.yaml, but I wasn't given access to that file. Without the actual weight values, I'll need to work with a default assumption of 1 per requirement unless specified otherwise.\n\nSo with 8 ready requirements and a weight limit of 4 per task, I can fit exactly 4 requirements per task. That gives me two tasks: R3.1 through R3.4 in the first, and R4.1 through R4.4 in the second. I also need to respect the constraint that each task can have at most 4 SRD sub-requirements, which these groupings satisfy.\n\nWith those 2 tasks allocated for tail, I have 2 remaining slots in my total task limit of 4. Looking ahead to the next release with ready requirements—07.1, which covers cp, mv, and rm—I'm seeing a lot of requirements to work with. The cp command alone has 16 requirements spread across four groups, mv has 15, and rm appears to have more that got cut off in the list.\n\nBut here's the issue: all three of these commands show \"not started\" status in the code. Before I can propose implementation tasks, I need to think about whether contract tasks are required first. The constraint mentions proposing contract tasks for new packages to define exported types and interfaces as Go stubs, but I'm wondering if that applies to cmd/ packages or mainly to pkg/ packages since these are utility implementations rather than libraries.\n\nLooking at cmd/tail specifically, it's just a main package with a main function and tests—there's nothing to export. The contract pattern seems more relevant for library packages that define reusable interfaces. I think the intent of the constraint is for pkg/ packages, not cmd/ binaries, since cmd packages don't have exported types or interfaces to stub out. I've got 4 task slots total, and tail is taking 2 of them for the current release. That leaves me 2 slots for cp in the next release, but cp has 16 requirements that would need 4 tasks to cover properly. I need to figure out which requirements to prioritize for those 2 remaining slots. I'm realizing I need to understand what each requirement actually entails before I can properly sequence these tasks, but I don't have access to the SRD files themselves. What I do know is that tail has partial implementation with R1-R2 already complete, leaving R3-R4 to implement, while cp is starting from scratch with all four requirement ranges needed.\n\nThe key insight is that my task descriptions can reference the SRD requirement IDs directly—the stitch agent will read those files to get the specific details. So I can structure tasks like \"Implement srd055-tail R3.1-R3.4\" and point to the SRD in the required_reading field, letting the agent handle the actual requirement details. I need to work within the constraints—I can't read the SRD files directly, but the stitch agent will handle that. So I'll structure the tasks to reference the specific requirement IDs and let the SRD provide the actual details, while basing my descriptions on what I can reasonably infer from the context about what tail should do.\n\nThere's a discrepancy between the code_status showing tail as \"not started\" and the requirement_states suggesting R1-R2 are already complete. Given the recent commits show other tasks were just completed in this run, the code likely exists even if the status snapshot is outdated. I should treat this as a modification task rather than creating from scratch.\n\nFor the remaining work, I only have tail left in 07.0, so after finishing R3.1-R3.4 for tail, I'll move into 07.1 which has cp, mv, and rm—all with their requirements ready to go.\n\nLet me map out the task sequence: tail gets split into two tasks covering R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4, each with a weight of 4. Then cp starts fresh with R1.1-R1.4, followed by R2.1-R2.4 depending on the first cp task. I should verify my weight calculations are correct and make sure I'm accounting for the assumption that all weights equal 1. For tail's R3 and R4 groups, I'm inferring these likely cover core functionality like handling multiple files and various output options based on what tail typically does.\n\nFor cp, the roadmap mentions file copying, recursive mode, attribute preservation, and symlink handling—so I'll structure the requirements around those areas. I'll keep the task descriptions generic but reference the specific SRD requirement IDs so the stitch agent can pull the actual details from the source documents. Looking at recent commit patterns, I should match the existing title format when I write these out. I'm laying out the task titles following the established pattern, then I need to structure the descriptions with the SRD references, file modifications, and acceptance criteria for each command implementation.\n\nFor tail, the files already exist from completed requirements, so I'm marking them for modification. For cp, R1 creates the initial files while R2 builds on them, so I need to track which files are created versus modified at each stage.\n\nLooking at the structure across both utilities with four requirement groups of four sub-requirements each, this suggests a progression from basic functionality through increasingly complex features.\n\nSince R1-R2 are already done, R3-R4 likely cover the more advanced capabilities. I'll keep my descriptions tied to the SRD rather than guessing specifics.\n\nFor the task descriptions, I need to make sure each one is self-contained—so when task 1 depends on task 0, I should explicitly note that the R3 requirements are already implemented as a foundation.\n\nNow I'm realizing the task requirements should map directly to the SRD requirement IDs. So if I'm assigning R3.1-R3.4 to a task, the requirements section should reference those exact SRD identifiers. The problem is I don't have the actual text of those requirements from the SRD, so I'll need to write them based on what makes sense for the tail command's feature set.\n\nLooking at the existing pattern in the prompt, it's acceptable to reference the SRD requirement by ID without spelling out the exact text—the stitch agent will read the SRD itself. But I should still provide context about which specific R-IDs to implement, what files to create or modify, and what patterns to follow so the agent knows the scope beyond just reading the spec.\n\nThe task requirements can simply reference the SRD IDs like \"Implement srd055-tail R3.1 as specified in the SRD,\" and I'll note the key design decisions: all cmd/ packages use differential testing, call the SIGPIPE handler, and reference the appropriate binaries like `gtail` and `gcp`.\n\nFor the required reading, I need to point to the SRD files at `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd055-tail.yaml` and `srd056-cp.yaml`, plus the existing source code, test utilities, and the sys package for understanding the SIGPIPE handler. I should also make sure all tests run with `_ALL=C` for consistency. I'm prioritizing the work across releases: tail needs two task groups covering requirements R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4, while cp has sixteen ready requirements that'll split into multiple batches starting with the first two task groups. The weight budget checks out assuming standard weights, so I can proceed with generating these tasks. No contract tasks needed for main packages, but I'm second-guessing myself on the strict interpretation—cmd/cp doesn't exist yet, so technically it might need a contract task first, even though a main package would have no exports to define.\n\nLooking at the constraint more carefully, it seems designed for pkg/ packages with actual exports, not cmd/ packages. The package_contracts section only lists pkg/ packages anyway, which confirms this interpretation. So I'll skip the contract task for cmd/cp and go straight to implementation.\n\nMy four tasks are: cmd/tail R3.1-R3.4, cmd/tail R4.1-R4.4, cmd/cp R1.1-R1.4, and cmd/cp R2.1-R2.4. Now I need to format these properly in YAML for the output.\n\nFor the tail tasks, I'm modifying the existing main.go file from the previous implementation, but for cp R1 I'll need to create the files from scratch.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":51612,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":51612},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dfbfc873-feed-4859-b155-7b99799259c9","uuid":"635268b0-9149-4231-8954-e81a3d8e8f3d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GET7uzeQd2zHjZ9HFRDduV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified by differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is stress-testing a spec-driven AI code generation pipeline (mage-claude-orchestrator), not shipping utilities.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/<name>/` holds one utility each (main.go + differential tests), `pkg/testutils/` provides the test harness, `pkg/sys/` wraps platform syscalls, `pkg/format/` handles output formatting, and `docs/specs/` contains the SRDs that drive generation.\n\n**Implementation state**: Releases 00.0 through 06.1 are partially or fully implemented. Many utilities are complete. The generation run (run44) is actively processing requirements in groups.\n\n**Current focus**: Release 07.0 — tr is implemented, sort has no ready requirements, tail has R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4 ready. Next is release 07.1 (cp, mv, rm) with all requirements ready.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\n1. **srd055-tail** (rel07.0) — 8 ready requirements: R3.1-R3.4, R4.1-R4.4 → 2 tasks\n2. **srd056-cp** (rel07.1) — 16 ready requirements: R1.1-R4.4, first 8 fit in 2 tasks\n3. Remaining cp (R3-R4), mv, rm deferred to next batch\n\n## Weight Budget (assuming weight=1 default — cannot read requirements.yaml per constraints)\n\n- Task 0: srd055 R3.1(1) + R3.2(1) + R3.3(1) + R3.4(1) = 4 ✓\n- Task 1: srd055 R4.1(1) + R4.2(1) + R4.3(1) + R4.4(1) = 4 ✓\n- Task 2: srd056 R1.1(1) + R1.2(1) + R1.3(1) + R1.4(1) = 4 ✓\n- Task 3: srd056 R2.1(1) + R2.2(1) + R2.3(1) + R2.4(1) = 4 ✓\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/tail: srd055 R3.1-R3.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd055-tail.yaml (requirement groups R3.1-R3.4)\n      - cmd/tail/main.go (existing implementation from R1-R2)\n      - cmd/tail/tail_test.go (existing differential tests from R1-R2)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (differential testing pattern -- RunDiffTests, BuildBinary, DiffTest)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler for SIGPIPE handling)\n      - CLAUDE.md (project conventions and build instructions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tail/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add functionality for srd055-tail requirements R3.1 through R3.4\n      - path: cmd/tail/tail_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test cases covering R3.1 through R3.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R3.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R3.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R3.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R3.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use the differential testing pattern: testutils.BuildBinary for the Go binary, exec.LookPath(\\\"gtail\\\") for the GNU reference, t.Skip if reference not found\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Set LC_ALL=C in all differential test environments\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Build with go build -o bin/tail ./cmd/tail/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"All R3.1-R3.4 behaviors from srd055-tail are implemented in cmd/tail/main.go\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Differential test cases exist for each R3.x requirement in cmd/tail/tail_test.go\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go build ./cmd/tail/ compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/tail/... -count=1 passes (or skips if gtail not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/tail/ reports no issues\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/tail: srd055 R4.1-R4.4\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd055-tail.yaml (requirement groups R4.1-R4.4)\n      - cmd/tail/main.go (existing implementation from R1-R3)\n      - cmd/tail/tail_test.go (existing differential tests from R1-R3)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (differential testing pattern -- RunDiffTests, BuildBinary, DiffTest)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler for SIGPIPE handling)\n      - CLAUDE.md (project conventions and build instructions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tail/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add functionality for srd055-tail requirements R4.1 through R4.4\n      - path: cmd/tail/tail_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test cases covering R4.1 through R4.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R4.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R4.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R4.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd055-tail requirement R4.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use the differential testing pattern: testutils.BuildBinary for the Go binary, exec.LookPath(\\\"gtail\\\") for the GNU reference, t.Skip if reference not found\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Set LC_ALL=C in all differential test environments\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Build with go build -o bin/tail ./cmd/tail/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"All R4.1-R4.4 behaviors from srd055-tail are implemented in cmd/tail/main.go\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Differential test cases exist for each R4.x requireme